    "result_text": "The build is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the per-city capacity rule for `POST /api/owners`: creating an owner is rejected with **409** when the owner's city already holds 50 or more owners.\n\nFollowing the existing `EnsureUniqueTelephone` / `EnsureUniqueHousehold` pattern, I added:\n\n1. **`EnsureCityCapacity`** (function) \u2014 reads the request DTO, counts existing owners in that city case-insensitively (the same comparison `AssignCustomerCode` uses), and throws when the count is at or above the `CAPACITY` limit (50).\n2. **`CityAtCapacityException`** (checked exception) \u2014 carries the city and limit for the message.\n3. **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler`** + escalation YAML \u2014 maps the exception to `HttpStatus.CONFLICT` (409).\n4. Wired `ensureCityCapacity` into `owners.POST.yml` after the uniqueness checks and before `normalizeEmail`, so it runs pre-build (no transaction governance, like the sibling validation steps).\n\nThe check lives in its own single-responsibility step, reuses the established counting/normalization idiom, and doesn't enlarge any existing class. The provided `AcceptanceTest` and the full suite pass.",
